---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import BaseTable from '../../components/BaseTable.astro';
import WorldSystemsTable from '../../components/WorldSystemsTable.astro';
import { guides } from '../../data/guides';

const guide = guides.find((g) => g.slug === 'number-system-conversion-table')!;

const toc = [
  { id: 'how-to-read', label: 'How to read the table' },
  { id: 'full-grid', label: '0–255 grid' },
  { id: 'powers', label: 'Powers of two' },
  { id: 'scale-table', label: 'Indian vs International' },
  { id: 'world-table', label: 'World systems' },
  { id: 'patterns', label: 'Patterns to memorise' },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is 255 in binary, octal and hexadecimal?',
    answer:
      '<strong>255 = 11111111₂ = 377₈ = FF₁₆.</strong> It is the largest value an 8-bit byte can hold, which is why RGB colour channels stop at 255.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How many lakh is 1 billion?',
    answer:
      '1 billion = <strong>10,000 lakh</strong> = 100 crore = 1 arab. A common error is to say 100 lakh, but 100 lakh is only 1 crore — ten times smaller than a billion.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What comes after crore in the Indian system?',
    answer:
      'After crore (10⁷) the Indian scale runs arab (10⁹), kharab (10¹¹), neel (10¹³), padma (10¹⁵) and shankh (10¹⁷). Because the steps are hundreds, each named unit is 100× the previous one.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why is the table grouped in four bases?',
    answer:
      'Binary, octal, decimal and hexadecimal are the four bases used in computing and digital logic. Each row lets you translate between them without arithmetic, and the powers-of-two table explains why the boundaries fall where they do.',
  },
];

const scaleRows = [
  ['10⁰', 'One', '1', 'One', '1'],
  ['10³', 'Thousand', '1,000', 'Thousand', '1,000'],
  ['10⁵', 'Hundred thousand', '100,000', 'Lakh', '1,00,000'],
  ['10⁶', 'Million', '1,000,000', 'Ten lakh', '10,00,000'],
  ['10⁷', 'Ten million', '10,000,000', 'Crore', '1,00,00,000'],
  ['10⁹', 'Billion', '1,000,000,000', 'Arab', '1,00,00,00,000'],
  ['10¹¹', 'Hundred billion', '100,000,000,000', 'Kharab', '1,00,00,00,00,000'],
  ['10¹²', 'Trillion', '1,000,000,000,000', 'Lakh crore', '1,00,000 crore'],
  ['10¹³', 'Ten trillion', '10,000,000,000,000', 'Neel', '1,00,00,00,00,00,000'],
  ['10¹⁵', 'Quadrillion', '1,000,000,000,000,000', 'Padma', '1,00,00,00,00,00,00,000'],
  ['10¹⁷', 'Hundred quadrillion', '100,000,000,000,000,000', 'Shankh', '1,00,00,00,00,00,00,00,000'],
];

const powers = [
  ['2⁰', '1'], ['2¹', '2'], ['2²', '4'], ['2³', '8'],
  ['2⁴', '16'], ['2⁵', '32'], ['2⁶', '64'], ['2⁷', '128'],
  ['2⁸', '256'], ['2⁹', '512'], ['2¹⁰', '1,024'], ['2¹¹', '2,048'],
  ['2¹²', '4,096'], ['2¹³', '8,192'], ['2¹⁴', '16,384'], ['2¹⁵', '32,768'],
  ['2¹⁶', '65,536'], ['2²⁰', '1,048,576'], ['2²⁴', '16,777,216'], ['2³⁰', '1,073,741,824'],
  ['2³²', '4,294,967,296'], ['2⁴⁰', '1,099,511,627,776'],
];
---

<GuideLayout guide={guide} toc={toc} faqs={faqs}>
  <h2 id="how-to-read">How to read a number system conversion table</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>number system conversion table</strong> is a lookup, not a lesson: each row holds one value written in several bases. Read across to translate, read down to see how a base rolls over. The grid below covers decimal, binary, octal and hexadecimal from 0 to 255 — the full range of a byte.
  </p>
  <div class="answer-box">
    <p><strong>Two rows worth memorising:</strong> 15 = <code>1111₂</code> = <code>17₈</code> = <code>F₁₆</code>, and 255 = <code>11111111₂</code> = <code>377₈</code> = <code>FF₁₆</code>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="full-grid">Number system conversion table, 0–255</h2>
  <p>
    Binary is shown as eight bits and hexadecimal as two digits, which is how they appear in code, colour values and memory dumps. Scroll the panel to reach the higher values.
  </p>
  <BaseTable to={255} caption="Values 0–255 in decimal, binary (8-bit), octal (3-digit) and hexadecimal (2-digit)." />

  <h2 id="powers">Powers of two</h2>
  <p>
    Binary boundaries are powers of two, so the table makes more sense with them alongside. The two most useful are 2¹⁰ = 1,024 and 2³² = 4,294,967,296.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Power</th><th>Value</th><th>Power</th><th>Value</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {powers.map((row, i) => (
        i % 2 === 0 && (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{row[0]}</code></td>
            <td>{row[1]}</td>
            <td><code>{powers[i + 1]?.[0] ?? ''}</code></td>
            <td>{powers[i + 1]?.[1] ?? ''}</td>
          </tr>
        )
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="scale-table">Indian vs International number system table</h2>
  <p>
    This is the table most people actually need when they search for a number system conversion table: the same power of ten, named in both systems, with the comma grouping shown.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Power</th><th>International name</th><th>International format</th><th>Indian name</th><th>Indian format</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {scaleRows.map((row) => (
        <tr>
          <td><code>{row[0]}</code></td>
          <td>{row[1]}</td>
          <td><code>{row[2]}</code></td>
          <td>{row[3]}</td>
          <td><code>{row[4]}</code></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note">
    The Indian system has no separate word at 10⁶: one million is “ten lakh”. That gap is why conversions between the two systems so often go wrong by a factor of ten.
  </p>

  <h2 id="world-table">How other countries name the same values</h2>
  <p>
    The International system uses the short scale: billion = 10⁹, trillion = 10¹². Much of continental Europe uses the long scale, where “billion” starts at 10¹². The table below benchmarks sixteen systems against the International standard.
  </p>
  <WorldSystemsTable caption="Number naming for 1 million, 1 billion and 1 trillion across sixteen systems, with the benchmark against the International short scale." />

  <h3>Nuance 1: Portuguese depends on the country</h3>
  <p>
    Portugal keeps the long scale: 1 billion = <em>mil milhões</em>, 1 trillion = <em>um bilião</em>. Brazil uses the short scale: 1 billion = <em>um bilhão</em>, 1 trillion = <em>um trilhão</em>. The same table row can therefore describe two different magnitudes.
  </p>

  <h3>Nuance 2: Hispanic “billón” is a trillion</h3>
  <p>
    Spanish says <em>mil millones</em> for 10⁹ and reserves <em>un billón</em> for 10¹². English 1 trillion = <em>un billón</em>; English 1 quintillion = <em>un trillón</em> (10¹⁸).
  </p>

  <h3>Nuance 3: French, German and Dutch keep a milliard</h3>
  <p>
    1 billion = <em>un milliard</em> / <em>eine Milliarde</em> / <em>een miljard</em> / <em>un miliardo</em>. Their “billion” (<em>billion</em>, <em>Billion</em>, <em>biljoen</em>, <em>bilione</em>) starts at 10¹², one step later than English.
  </p>

  <h3>Nuance 4: Japanese, Chinese and Korean count in ten-thousands</h3>
  <p>
    1 billion = 10億 / 十亿 / 십억; 1 trillion = 兆 / 兆 / 조. The grouping is four digits, so a table built for three-digit groups will misalign every row.
  </p>

  <h3>Nuance 5: India has its own column</h3>
  <p>
    1 billion = 1 arab = 100 crore; 1 trillion = 1 lakh crore. Indian grouping is 3-then-2, so the comma count is higher than International for the same digits.
  </p>

  <h2 id="patterns">Patterns worth memorising</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Every hex digit is four bits.</strong> <code>F</code> = <code>1111</code>, <code>A</code> = <code>1010</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Every octal digit is three bits.</strong> <code>7</code> = <code>111</code>, <code>5</code> = <code>101</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Powers of two end in a doubling pattern:</strong> 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256.</li>
    <li><strong>Indian grouping is 3 then 2s:</strong> 12,34,56,789, not 123,456,789.</li>
    <li><strong>1 million = 10 lakh; 1 billion = 1 arab = 100 crore.</strong> Derive every other conversion from these two anchors.</li>
    <li><strong>“Billion” is not universal.</strong> In Spanish, French and European Portuguese it means 10¹²; in English and Brazilian Portuguese it means 10⁹.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    For printable versions of these tables, download the <a href="/knowledge-catalog/number-system-conversion-pdf/">number system conversion PDF</a>. For the method behind each cell, start with the <a href="/knowledge-catalog/number-system-converter-with-steps/">converter with steps</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
